# These files are not read by the host. They are sent to a Linux guest and run
# there, so their line endings are part of whether they work, not a preference.
#
# scripts.js reads each one off disk and passes it through byte for byte -- that
# is deliberate, so a script stays valid shell you can copy onto a machine and
# run by hand. It also means whatever git checked out is exactly what the guest
# executes. On Windows core.autocrlf=true rewrites LF to CRLF ON CHECKOUT, so a
# fresh clone would serve `#!/bin/bash\r` and the guest would answer `bad
# interpreter: /bin/bash^M` -- a failure that appears only in a clone, never on
# the machine the files were written on, and never in the diff.
#
# The blobs are stored LF already. This keeps the working copy that way too.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# The seed the unattended installer is handed, for the same reason.
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# EVERYTHING IN A provision FOLDER, whatever it is called.
#
# The rules above go by extension, and four of the files here have none that
# matches while still being RUN on the guest:
#
#   autoinstall-user-data   no extension at all. It is the seed the unattended
#                           installer is handed, and its late-commands run
#                           `/bin/bash` -- so carriage returns land inside the
#                           command strings.
#   okc-mcp.js              `#!/usr/bin/env node`, and so are okc-only-hook.js
#                           and okc-credential.js. A shebang with a carriage
#                           return is `bad interpreter: ...^M`, the same failure
#                           the note above describes for /bin/bash.
#
# `git ls-files --eol provision/` showed `attr/` empty for all six, which is how
# this was found. They are LF in the index and LF in this working copy, so
# nothing is broken HERE -- and with core.autocrlf=true a fresh clone would
# check them out CRLF, which is precisely the failure that appears only in a
# clone and never in a diff.
#
# By folder rather than by name, so a file added later is covered by having been
# put in the right place rather than by somebody remembering this file exists.
dashboard/provision/* text eol=lf
dashboard-beta/src/app/vms/provision/scripts/* text eol=lf

# AND THE FILES A JOB IS HANDED, for the same reason one folder up.
#
# These are .js, so the extension rules above do not pin them and git's default
# would check them out CRLF on a fresh clone. They are read as TEXT here and
# written into a guest through a heredoc, then run by the guest's node — so a
# carriage return does not stay a formatting detail, it travels.
#
# The immediate cost is smaller than a shebang with a `^M` in it, because node
# tolerates CRLF in a .js file. What does not tolerate it is anything that
# compares a line: `watch-guest.js` parses claude's stream-json a line at a
# time, and a trailing `\r` on every line is the kind of fault that produces
# almost-right output rather than an error.
#
# By folder, like the one above, so a payload added later is covered by being
# put in the right place rather than by somebody remembering this file exists.
dashboard-beta/src/app/vms/dispatch/guest/* text eol=lf

# Git hooks, which have no extension by design -- git looks for `pre-receive`,
# not `pre-receive.sh`. So the rules above miss them entirely, and the first
# clone would hand git a shell script full of carriage returns. This one runs on
# the HOST rather than in a guest, and it is what stops a push reaching master,
# so it failing to run is the most expensive version of this mistake available.
dashboard/repos/hooks/* text eol=lf
